<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_search — magnifying glass (search field start icon)
    ===
    Referenced by fragment_chats.xml:
        app:startIconDrawable="@drawable/ic_search"
        app:startIconTint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single logical glyph, single fill color (#FFFFFFFF).
       The TextInputLayout's startIconTint determines the
       actual rendered color at runtime. Do NOT change the
       fill to a brand color — it would be overridden by the
       tint and would only mislead future maintainers.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT, matching the Material Design search
       icon standard size. TextInputLayout sizes its start
       icon to 24dp by default; using a different viewport
       would either stretch the icon or render it as a
       sub-region of a larger canvas, producing a visual
       mismatch with the 16sp hint text in the field.

    3. STANDARD GLYPH — this is the canonical Material
       Design magnifying-glass shape (a lens ring plus a
       diagonal handle at the lower-right). The path data
       below is byte-for-byte identical to the shape shipped
       in Google's own material-icons package for the
       "search" icon. Search is one of the few glyphs where
       deviation from the standard hurts rather than helps:
       users recognize the shape instantly, and any
       "creative" variation reads as a bug. This is a
       deliberate non-original design decision.

    4. TWO CONTOURS, OPPOSITE WINDINGS.
       The path contains the outer silhouette (trace of the
       lens ring plus handle) and the inner circle of the
       lens. The two contours wind in OPPOSITE directions so
       that Android's default nonZero fill rule produces the
       expected "ring + handle" shape with a transparent
       lens interior. This is not always obvious from the
       XML — the fill type is not explicitly declared, and
       the behavior depends on the source winding directions
       as rendered by Google's own file. Do NOT "simplify"
       by adding fillType="evenOdd" — it works, but it
       changes the anti-aliasing behavior of the ring edge
       by a sub-pixel amount, and the standard Material
       search icon is the one shape where matching Google's
       reference rendering exactly is worth preserving.

    5. NO android:tint ON THE VECTOR.
       Adding a tint at the vector level would be applied
       by the framework at draw time, then the
       TextInputLayout's startIconTint would apply on top of
       that. The result is visually correct, but the double
       tint produces a subtle aliasing fringe on some GPU
       drivers. The correct pattern is: fill = white
       template, tint = single application at the view
       level, nothing at the vector level. This rule applies
       to every monochrome icon in the project — do not
       deviate for this one.

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored ATTRIBUTE.
       The magnifying glass is not directional. Auto-mirroring
       under RTL would produce no visible change (mirroring a
       symmetric glyph is a no-op), so the attribute would be
       dead weight. Only add it to icons where mirroring
       produces a visible improvement (e.g., back/forward
       arrows, send button arrows, list-item chevrons).
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Single path with two closed contours:
          - Outer silhouette: lens ring + diagonal handle
          - Inner circle: the lens interior (rendered as
            transparent because the windings cancel under
            nonZero)
        Rendered result: a clean magnifying glass that reads
        correctly at 16dp (small icon, e.g. in a dense text
        field) through 32dp (large icon, e.g. in a
        full-screen search overlay) — this is one of the few
        icons that scales cleanly across that range.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M15.5,14h-0.79l-0.28,-0.27C15.41,12.59 16,11.11 16,9.5 16,5.91 13.09,3 9.5,3S3,5.91 3,9.5 5.91,16 9.5,16c1.61,0 3.09,-0.59 4.23,-1.57l0.27,0.28v0.79l5,4.99L20.49,19l-4.99,-5zM9.5,14C7.01,14 5,11.99 5,9.5S7.01,5 9.5,5 14,7.01 14,9.5 11.99,14 9.5,14z" />

</vector>
